  <!-- ---------- the problem, and the two ways of answering it ---------- -->
  <section class="cst-sec" data-chrome="light" aria-labelledby="prob-h">
    <div class="cst-shell cst-shell--text">
      <h2 class="cst-h2 cst-mark cst-mark--coral" id="prob-h">the problem</h2>
      <p class="cst-q" style="margin-top:.85em">The drop-off happens on the screen before the session.</p>
      <p class="cst-p">Someone opening the app anxious at 11pm is asked to browse categories, compare durations and read descriptions. That is cognitive work, and it competes directly with the state they are trying to leave.</p>
      <p class="cst-p">Choice is presented as generosity. For a stressed user, a large library is friction.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cst-shell">
      <div class="cst-models">
        <div class="cst-model cst-model--out">
          <h3 class="cst-h3">the library model</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>The user must self-diagnose, then translate that into a search</li>
            <li>Every option looks equally valid, so none feels correct</li>
            <li>Decision cost is highest exactly when the user can least pay it</li>
            <li>Success depends on already knowing what you need</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cst-model cst-model--in">
          <h3 class="cst-h3">solution</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Two taps, a feeling and a duration, and the system decides</li>
            <li>One recommendation, with its reasoning stated in plain language</li>
            <li>Time of day is inferred, never asked</li>
            <li>The app carries the expertise so the user doesn't have to</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ---------- the engine ----------
       Small enough to say out loud, which was the constraint rather than the
       outcome. The mudra beside it is the photograph with the notes still on
       it: what the gesture is for is the thing the rules are matching to. -->
  <section class="cst-sec" data-chrome="light" aria-labelledby="eng-h">
    <div class="cst-shell">
      <div class="cst-aside">
        <div>
          <h2 class="cst-h2 cst-mark cst-mark--sun" id="eng-h">small enough to explain</h2>
          <p class="cst-p" style="margin-top:.85em">A feeling maps to a mudra. Time of day acts as a corrective layer. If the clock says it's late and the mapped mudra is an energising one, the system swaps it for a wind-down gesture, and tells the user why. Nothing sits behind a black box, so every recommendation carries its own justification.</p>
        </div>
        <img src="../assets/img/nirvana2/nv2-hand-notes.webp"
             alt="A bronze hand held in Gyan mudra, annotated by hand: boosts memory, improves sharpness and focus, clears mental fog, improves sleep."
             width="812" height="686" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The engine in full, drawn rather than rebuilt in markup. Five rules and
       three inputs are the entire model, and a tidy vector version of them
       would claim more machinery than there is. -->
  <section class="cst-sec cst-sec--tight" data-chrome="light" aria-labelledby="dia-h">
    <div class="cst-shell">
      <h2 class="sr-only" id="dia-h">The recommendation engine</h2>
      <img class="cst-diagram" src="../assets/img/nirvana2/nv2-engine.webp"
           alt="Three inputs, feeling, time of day and available time, enter a set of five matching rules: feeling to mudra, use the default if none, late-hour override, duration or inferred, state the reason. A late-hour override swaps energising for wind-down. They resolve to one recommendation: Gyan Mudra, the gesture of knowledge, chosen because the user is stressed this morning, for five minutes, tagged focus and clarity. Written underneath: five rules is the whole model. What a user cannot follow, a user cannot trust."
           width="2250" height="916" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The second band arrived flat, water and devices together, so it goes
       down whole. -->
  <section class="cst-band" data-chrome="dark">
    <img src="../assets/img/nirvana2/nv2-screens.webp"
         alt="Three Nirvana screens over a lake: choosing how you feel from a set of plain-language chips, choosing how much time you have, and the resulting Shuni Mudra practice with the instruction to touch the tip of your middle finger to your thumb."
         width="2400" height="1257" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  </section>

  <!-- ---------- the decisions, next to what they produced ----------
       Three calls on the left, the thing they add up to on the right. The
       prototype is not a picture of the argument, it is the argument, so it
       holds its place while the reasoning scrolls past it. -->
  <section class="cst-sec" data-chrome="light" id="prototype" aria-labelledby="dec-h">
    <div class="cst-shell">
      <div class="cst-dec">
        <div>
          <div class="cst-ruled">
            <h2 class="cst-h2" id="dec-h">design decisions taken</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="cst-opts">
            <div class="cst-opt">
              <h3 class="cst-h3">ask for a feeling</h3>
              <p>Onboarding collects plain-language states, overthinking, burnout, poor sleep, because that is the vocabulary users already have. Translating that into a practice is the product's job, not the user's. A category list would have been easier to build and would have moved the work back onto the person least able to do it.</p>
              <span class="cst-verdict cst-verdict--in">built</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cst-opt">
              <h3 class="cst-h3">showing the reasoning</h3>
              <p>Every recommendation carries a sentence naming the inputs that produced it. Stated reasoning is what turns an algorithmic guess into something a user will follow, and it constrains the model, because a rule you can't explain in one line doesn't get to exist.</p>
              <span class="cst-verdict cst-verdict--in">built</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cst-opt">
              <h3 class="cst-h3">the hand gestures (mudras)</h3>
              <p>Schematic vector hands render at any size, need no localisation, and highlight only the contact points that matter. Ten of them cover the emotional range onboarding asks about. Video was the assumed default and would have cost a production pipeline, a re-shoot for every addition, and a comprehension problem across cultures that Part 1 had already measured.</p>
              <span class="cst-verdict cst-verdict--in">built</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- The prototype itself, running in the page. It covers onboarding,
             the feeling and duration steps, the engine, the guided player with
             its breathing rhythm, and the profile. The session timer runs at
             four times speed so a fifteen-minute practice finishes here. -->
        <div class="cst-proto">
          <div class="cst-proto-stage">
            <iframe src="prototype/" title="Nirvana 2.0 interactive prototype" loading="lazy"></iframe>
          </div>
          <p class="cst-proto-note">It is real, and it works. Tap through it.<br>
            <a href="prototype/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Or open it full size in a new tab</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ---------- what the two parts add up to ---------- -->
  <section class="cst-sec" data-chrome="light" aria-labelledby="out-h">
    <div class="cst-shell cst-shell--text">
      <h2 class="cst-h2 cst-mark cst-mark--sun" id="out-h">logic is real and testable</h2>
      <p class="cst-p" style="margin-top:.85em">Part 1 proved reinforcement brings people back. Part 2 removes the obstacle that met them when they arrived. Together they make a single argument. <strong>Retention is a design problem at both ends of the session, not just at the notification.</strong></p>

      <p class="cst-caveat"><b>NOT YET MEASURED.</b> The prototype is functional and the logic is testable, but this has not run with a user cohort. The next honest step is a two-week diary study comparing matched entry against library entry. That is the study that would either confirm this or kill it.</p>

      <div class="cst-btns">
        <a class="cst-btn cst-btn--line" href="../nirvana/">read part 1</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

